From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: a bSTA record belongs to the radio that names it
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

The last of the five ghost mechanisms, and the only one still open.

A bSTA record is created by handle_backhaul_sta_caps_report() through
cntlr_add_sta() with no node_add_sta() behind it. So nref stays 0 for the
life of the record; sta_dec_ref() is the only place that arms the ageout
timer (sta.c:415-429) and it is never reached. The record cannot expire. Our
993 sweeps its row out of mapc.db, which is the symptom - the struct sta in
c->sta_table stays until the process ends.

The temptation is to arm a timer. That is the wrong tool twice over.
stale_sta_timeout is 20 days on these boxes, so the record would go from
immortal to three-weeks-old and nothing measurable would change. And a bSTA
is not a client that walks out of range, where time is the only evidence
there is: its absence is *stated*. The radio names exactly one bSTA and
names it again in every Backhaul STA Capability Report, which is the report
that calls this function.

So the record's owner is the radio, and it is freed where the radio stops
naming it - the same shape as mapc_sync_bss_sweep(), which drops the rows a
radio no longer reports. Deterministic, at the moment it becomes true,
instead of three weeks later.

Guarded twice: only a record marked is_bsta, and only one with nref == 0. If
anything took a reference in the meantime, it is not ours to free.

NOT covered, and said out loud rather than left to be discovered: a radio
that stops having a bSTA at all does not call this function, so that record
still waits for the node to go. Closing that needs the caller to say "no
bSTA" as clearly as it says "this bSTA" - a follow-up, not a silent gap.

Verify: on a mesh where an agent re-associates its backhaul with a new bSTA
MAC (a reflash does this), the controller log must show "no longer reports
bSTA", and `select count(*) from sta where is_bsta=1` must match the number
of backhaul links actually up - not grow across reflashes as it does today.
--- a/src/cntlr.c
+++ b/src/cntlr.c
@@ -931,6 +931,36 @@
 
 int cntlr_radio_add_bsta(struct controller *c, struct netif_radio *r, uint8_t *macaddr)
 {
+	uint8_t *old = r->radio_el->bsta.macaddr;
+
+	/* A radio names exactly one bSTA, and it names it again in every
+	 * Backhaul STA Capability Report. So when the name changes, the record
+	 * for the previous one has lost its owner - and it cannot leave on its
+	 * own: a bSTA is created by cntlr_add_sta() with no node_add_sta()
+	 * behind it, so nref stays 0, sta_dec_ref() is never reached and the
+	 * ageout timer is never armed (sta.c:415-429). The record is immortal.
+	 *
+	 * Waiting for a timer would be the wrong tool even if one were armed.
+	 * stale_sta_timeout is 20 days on these boxes, and unlike a client that
+	 * walks out of range - where time is the only evidence there is - a bSTA
+	 * never disappears quietly: its absence is stated, in the very report
+	 * that carries this call. So drop it where that is said, the way
+	 * mapc_sync_bss_sweep() drops the rows a radio no longer reports.
+	 *
+	 * Guarded twice: only a bSTA, and only one nobody holds. If anything
+	 * took a reference in the meantime it is not ours to free.
+	 */
+	if (!hwaddr_is_zero(old) && memcmp(old, macaddr, 6)) {
+		struct sta *prev = cntlr_find_sta(c->sta_table, old);
+
+		if (prev && prev->is_bsta && prev->nref == 0) {
+			info("%s: radio " MACFMT " no longer reports bSTA "
+			     MACFMT ", dropping it\n", __func__,
+			     MAC2STR(r->radio_el->macaddr), MAC2STR(old));
+			cntlr_del_sta(c->sta_table, prev);
+		}
+	}
+
 	memcpy(r->radio_el->bsta.macaddr, macaddr, 6);
 	return 0;
 }
